## Stack decisions

| Decision | Choice | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Runtime | Node 24 LTS, TypeScript (ESM), run with `tsx` in dev and `tsc` for the build | Two developers get one language and typed SQL results. Node 24 is the current LTS line. |
| HTTP framework | **Fastify 5** | It does schema validation and OpenAPI generation natively, has good built-in logging (pino), and handles far more than a few hundred requests per minute. Express would need extra libraries for validation and typing. |
| Validation and types | **TypeBox** with `@fastify/type-provider-typebox` | One schema gives request validation, TypeScript types and OpenAPI. There's no duplicated DTO layer. |
| Database | **PostgreSQL 17** | Books, authors and stock are relational. Foreign keys, unique constraints and `CHECK (stock >= 0)` protect the data. Postgres also handles concurrent stock updates correctly. |
| DB access | **`pg` plus hand-written SQL** (parameterised) behind small repository functions, with no ORM | The schema has three or four tables. Plain SQL is easy to review, and stock updates need exact SQL (`UPDATE … SET qty = qty + $1`). An ORM adds migrations and magic the team doesn't need yet. If the team prefers a typed builder later, Kysely is the step up. |
| Migrations | **node-pg-migrate**, plain SQL files, forward-only | Migrations are versioned in git and run in CI and production by the same command. |
| Testing | `node:test` plus Fastify `app.inject()` against a **real Postgres** (docker compose service, one schema/database per test run) | There's no need for Jest. Mocking the DB would hide the SQL bugs most likely to occur, such as constraints and race conditions. |
| Lint and format | ESLint (typescript-eslint) and Prettier | Standard setup that both developers will already know. |
| Package manager | npm (or pnpm if you already use it) | Fewer tools for two people. The lockfile is committed. |
| Config | Env vars parsed and validated at startup with a TypeBox schema, `.env.example` committed | The app fails fast on missing config. |
| Auth | A static API key in the `Authorization: Bearer` header, compared in constant time, from env | This is an internal API with no client yet. Proper user auth can wait until a client exists. It must not be left off, because the service holds business data. |
| Deployment | One `Dockerfile` and one `docker-compose.yml` for local Postgres | A single stateless process, and no queue, cache or Redis. At this load none of those are justified. |
| Observability | pino JSON logs, `/healthz` and `/readyz` (the latter runs `SELECT 1`) | This is enough to run a service at this size. |

**Deliberately left out:** Redis, queues, GraphQL, microservices, DI containers, a repository/service/controller layering ceremony, and an ORM. Each can be added when a real need appears.

## Domain and API shape

- `authors` and `books` are many-to-many, because books can have several authors. A book has an ISBN-13 that is unique.
- Stock is a **separate table** (`stock`, one row per book) with a movement log (`stock_movements`). Counts are changed through adjustments such as `+20 received` or `-1 sold`, not by overwriting the number. This gives an audit trail and avoids lost updates.

```
GET    /healthz  /readyz
GET    /v1/authors            POST /v1/authors
GET    /v1/authors/:id        PATCH /v1/authors/:id
GET    /v1/books?author=&q=&limit=&cursor=
POST   /v1/books              GET /v1/books/:id
PATCH  /v1/books/:id          DELETE /v1/books/:id
GET    /v1/books/:id/stock
POST   /v1/books/:id/stock-adjustments   {delta, reason}
```

- Lists use cursor pagination (`limit` and `cursor`), with a hard maximum of 100.
- Errors use one shape, `{ error: { code, message } }`, with 400, 404, 409 and 422 used consistently.
- 409 is returned on a duplicate ISBN, and 422 on a stock adjustment that would go below zero.

